  "Name": "Vasicek1953-mirror-n2-k5",
  "Source": "A. Vasicek, \"Multiple thin dielectric films as mirrors and interference filters,\" Czech. J. Phys. 3, 206-222 (1953). doi:10.1007/BF01687127 - Sec. II a-b, pp. 208-212 (Eqs. 19, 24-34'); Table II p. 214; Table IV lower part p. 222",
  "Notes": "Same quarter-wave H/L mirror on glass (k = 5) but with the incident medium of low index n2 = 1.36 (the reflectance r_k^2 of Sec. II and the mirror half of the interference filter)\nSpecification: Reflectance r_k^2 of H L H L H | glass seen from a medium of index 1.36\nReported: Normal incidence from the low-index medium n2 = 1.36 onto film H; reflectance r_k^2 (fraction). Table II (p. 214), exact (multiple-reflection) values: x=60 deg (1650 nm) 0.00717, x=90 deg (1100 nm) 0.11664, x=120 deg (825 nm) 0.09424; the paper's single-reflection approximation gives 0.00265, 0.12718, 0.00265. Table IV lower part (p. 222): r_k^2 = 0.86254 at x = 180 deg (550 nm); Fabry-Perot half-width 2*dLambda = 259 A from Eq. (48) using this r^2 (spacer of index n2). Fig. 4 (p. 213) plots r_k^2 vs k for x = 60-180 deg (plot only).\nNotes: The paper treats the incident medium as a semi-infinite medium of index n2 (film B / cryolite), i.e. the reflectance inside a filter at the spacer boundary. Here it is modelled as an incident medium of constant n = 1.36. Same index and x-to-wavelength assumptions as the air designs.\nLayers from Sec. II a-b, pp. 208-212 (Eqs. 19, 24-34'); Table II p. 214; Table IV lower part p. 222; layer 1 faces the incident medium.\nChecked with ThinFilmDesigner (2026-10-21), agrees with the paper: ours R 0.862521 at 550 nm; paper R 0.86254 (printed).",
